   "Some works ask something of you. The Red Sea Act was one of them, a wound and a ritual of healing at the same time.",
   "It started as an image I could not shake: a woman bound by black ropes, floating in open water, tied to a circle of men. It was hard to describe and harder to ask people to step into. The act it answered was much heavier. The ties between men and women, between history and now, are not always ones we choose. And yet there we were, walking into the water together, choosing to face it.",
   "It was a process, an unspoken agreement to hold and be held. Slowly, with caution and care, we built a space where vulnerability could meet strength. When the first light touched the water and everyone stepped in, something shifted. The idea became a ritual. We took our places in the sea. Then, stillness.",
   "Afterwards we sat together, quiet, the salt drying on our skin. Someone sang. Then we spoke. What was said in that circle stays in that circle, but I carry it with me. To make art that moves pain instead of only showing it is a privilege.",
